  "comments": {
    "bugs": {
      "19307": {
        "comments": [
          {
            "count": 0,
            "is_markdown": false,
            "text": "Package C code that sticks to the API can still get at promise objects, which the work in bug 18928 set out to keep away from packages (comment 7 there). The `...` binding is a DOTSXP whose CARs are promises, and R_getVarEx(R_DotsSymbol, env, FALSE, ...) returns it, as does any .Call argument built from get(\"...\"), env$... or as.list(env, all.names = TRUE). CAR() of it is a PROMSXP, and CAR/CDR/TAG on that read the value, code and environment, sidestepping the non-API PRVALUE/PRCODE/PRENV.\n\nReproducible example: attached as dots-promise-exposure-repro.R (it compiles a small .Call library); every route reports \"promise\", where the Dots API never hands one out. Checked on r90880.\n\nThis is used on CRAN: filearray walks the R_getVarEx() result with CAR() and collects the promises into a list, and this.path evaluates (function(...) get(\"...\"))(NULL) from C and reads the promise fields to obtain R_UnboundValue.\n\nHaving R_getVarEx() refuse `...` would not close this, since the R-level routes remain, and it would break rlang's env_clone(), which copies a frame's `...` through R_getVar() without opening it. Closing it seems to need the package-facing CAR/CDR/TAG (and setters) to reject DOTSXP and PROMSXP, which would break the two packages above, so I'm raising it as a design question without a patch.",
            "bug_id": 19307,
